LeetCode 148 — Sort List
Problem Understanding

You are given the head of a singly linked list. Sort the linked list in ascending order.

Example:

Input:
4 → 2 → 1 → 3

Output:
1 → 2 → 3 → 4

The important constraint is that the expected optimal solution should achieve:

O(n log n) time
O(1) auxiliary space for the fully optimized iterative version

Because linked lists don't support random access efficiently, Merge Sort is a natural fit.

Approaches

We can solve it from brute force to optimal:

Convert to ArrayList + Sort — O(n log n), O(n)
Insertion Sort on Linked List — O(n²), O(1)
Recursive Merge Sort — O(n log n), O(log n) recursion
Bottom-Up Iterative Merge Sort — O(n log n), O(1) ⭐

Your supplied code is Approach 3: Recursive Merge Sort.

Approach 1 — ArrayList + Collections.sort()
Idea

Since sorting an array is easy:

Traverse the linked list.
Store all values in an ArrayList.
Sort the array.
Either create a new list or overwrite the existing node values.

This is simple but uses extra memory.

Code

class Solution {
    public ListNode sortList(ListNode head) {

        ArrayList<Integer> arr = new ArrayList<>();

        ListNode curr = head;

        while (curr != null) {
            arr.add(curr.val);
            curr = curr.next;
        }

        Collections.sort(arr);

        curr = head;
        int i = 0;

        while (curr != null) {
            curr.val = arr.get(i++);
            curr = curr.next;
        }

        return head;
    }
}

Dry Run

Input:

4 → 2 → 1 → 3

Store:

[4, 2, 1, 3]

Sort:

[1, 2, 3, 4]

Put values back:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4
Complexity
Time: O(n log n)
Space: O(n)

The sorting time is good, but the extra array is unnecessary.

Approach 2 — Insertion Sort
Idea

We can sort the linked list directly using Insertion Sort.

Maintain a sorted portion:

sorted → 1 → 4

Take the next node:

3

Find its correct position:

1 → 3 → 4

Then continue.

This works nicely with linked lists because inserting a node doesn't require shifting elements like an array.

Code

class Solution {
    public ListNode sortList(ListNode head) {

        if (head == null || head.next == null) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);

        ListNode curr = head;

        while (curr != null) {

            ListNode next = curr.next;

            ListNode prev = dummy;

            while (prev.next != null &&
                   prev.next.val < curr.val) {
                prev = prev.next;
            }

            curr.next = prev.next;
            prev.next = curr;

            curr = next;
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
}

